#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130300} :activity/id #uuid "bf654963-a5d1-5562-85bc-b7c2fbbe6d40" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130449 :tutorial/content "Recall that when we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi y \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we have a parametrically defined curve: \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in \\left[t_1,t_2\\right].\n$$\n\nIn the case of a parametric curve, the arc length element $\\textrm{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following formula for the surface area generated by rotating a parametrically defined curve about the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "36a57433-1c78-58b8-860c-0ea6e3471761" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6113 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c5c27e9e-03cb-5a93-8a56-bd33588960fe" :step/math-academy-id 618749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130466 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f849fe7f-670f-5a7f-8dc7-dbbd33a876f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7707 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "031d5fb3-1834-595b-8a36-577430a0f9a7" :step/math-academy-id 618750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129073 :question/id #uuid "066f76be-83f6-52fd-ab3f-00786d18f64e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9866"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050860}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541085}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"} :step/id #uuid "a6a3e1f3-c536-58d3-a81e-7966262884ce" :step/math-academy-id 618751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130450 :tutorial/content "When we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi x \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in the case of a parametrically defined curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "548401c3-a52c-5444-b834-57c50c4bfa72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6145 :tutorial/title "Surface Area Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "622390da-a9e6-55af-8449-1ceed5d4cec3" :step/math-academy-id 618754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129319 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129074 :question/id #uuid "b7f404a3-8885-5a33-9cfb-1e12a6c745a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9867"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049491}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530457}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "ddd7ced1-9fe0-533d-bd7a-9dfbcff663f8" :step/math-academy-id 618755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129075 :question/id #uuid "205f4d6b-affa-530e-979b-a394e7a46bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554602}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "2f4e64a8-3397-5e7e-b805-080c59c56440" :step/math-academy-id 618758}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129340}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530558} :activity/id #uuid "f6065dfd-4199-50b0-9334-da4b9f494b0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6878ad2d-c3f6-5472-b932-d7a4364f9bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{4}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529841} {:answer/id #uuid "735f35d2-93c2-528a-acc9-d6a1cf48503f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} {:answer/id #uuid "8f18c374-0ec5-5b5a-8aa2-bf73f6a415be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{2}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529926} {:answer/id #uuid "b4866bd0-791b-5423-befb-168b1ad8cf39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530030} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c2df3-9d88-53e0-973d-199d14e0ed36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{4}{2}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4a256d7-74ef-5f59-aa17-d233ba137369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5349d9de-8c6d-5416-a022-6878977b6004" :question/math-academy-id "q-139057" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y={2}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "032c215a-e883-53c2-bdc0-c14d20b9401e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3256b67-7e99-5826-a95a-b55a197e4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184} :answer-field/id #uuid "12fa28de-d131-50b4-b0e1-7dd696b0e54f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f72f786-eb31-5b03-9051-ae70858e9f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-352035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt[4]{6}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "08362200-3b6f-52bb-9fff-49cbac1aba34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15a0040-721e-5583-80c4-6b4b6d161036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi(2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b596d95-5abf-5a25-8619-bd46ab687c7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "95891d65-20e2-5732-9bd4-e52bbf49b5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352088" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a2cc474-bbd9-524c-88cd-2cfdbe4a121b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276f10df-f807-54a9-b01f-7cf1b357ec66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529665} {:answer/id #uuid "8f170c4b-c5f7-5356-8a5a-6fd851ea13ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529925} {:answer/id #uuid "a53f81c5-8ba3-5b6b-9818-2872dc69946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529990} {:answer/id #uuid "b86bdc8d-5bf9-5ea7-8414-b3dab58e55bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530042} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f6ac1e-5d34-5fa1-a3ce-c20f5afa43d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529990} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a8fb9e3-9863-5703-95c1-0d8295fc07d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "497a1af7-923f-5a2e-82d5-147a5d2a1d6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-138945" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "50c04e39-0863-56d5-a574-6b3bc23fdf01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961eb0d5-8253-50e6-a961-755f2db89486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529944}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529944} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f06b1d-08c6-50bc-8914-e903872c2d7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6528b016-7e7a-5f42-bb80-0fc7063586be" :question/math-academy-id "q-352031" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}t)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{2}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(4)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d943a17-8038-5310-ba12-619cf9e0f21c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530550}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529573}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532757} :activity/id #uuid "15816cbf-9c2c-53ef-a829-ca2321defe44" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fd56eb4-4c51-5eff-a136-8a8cbe4d2081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531963} {:answer/id #uuid "26b58db8-aa9b-5306-acc7-0ae354e19ac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532013} {:answer/id #uuid "d30855cf-2561-551f-9f08-0885a43147ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{63\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532385} {:answer/id #uuid "e76641f1-4b1e-5f93-a7e4-384fd90837d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{24\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532432} {:answer/id #uuid "e78f34b4-d5a7-5009-8033-283511cf7d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{56\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532013} :answer-field/id #uuid "fded47ac-9666-5e9a-a118-02ceca041d7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "294bf3af-6f1c-5f27-8904-a3e6a8a75f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-138970" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t-{t}^{3},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{3})=3-3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(3-3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(6t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9-18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{48\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "57085c22-32d0-5a83-a69e-4ce3a9bdecbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d11b877-fb53-589c-8eea-da397cb6e4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532095} {:answer/id #uuid "9873e93d-4b80-59cd-bde4-4c4887e98e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{9}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532281} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7fb2c2-362a-55ce-b957-f29bf7d97ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532407} {:answer/id #uuid "e033b1bb-f523-513b-b42b-df8a9a20816a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532412} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4836d1-dcb7-5a07-b0af-0193ffb18029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532095} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b1becb6-0d80-5be3-8b07-052ebc9be8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bec28ae8-3800-5ebe-aea9-25248c908bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139031" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-3t,\\,t\\in [-\\sqrt{3},0].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3t)=3{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{{(6t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2}-3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}-18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{9({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)|{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{3}-3t)({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{5}-2{t}^{3}-3t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =6\\pi {\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0}({t}^{5}-2{t}^{3}-3t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{6}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{0} \\\\  & =6\\pi [0-(\\frac{27}{6}-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{9}{2})] \\\\  & =6\\pi \\cdot \\frac{27}{6} \\\\  & =27\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "64958667-b549-5eb7-8561-a3974fe10b64" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e381238e-91d4-5561-a74f-947fada01bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532419}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532419} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1dc858-f317-5a50-ac0a-63bd26d09cc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "568d7106-115a-5160-be7c-9ea466db4771" :question/math-academy-id "q-352091" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{[-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)]}^{2}+{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)=1$ and ${sec}^{2}⁡(t)-1={tan}^{2}⁡(t)$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+[{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)]}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡(t)-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\sqrt{{tan}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot |\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)]_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-2\\pi [\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "679ce636-9dcd-5b15-ab39-bb3b7ed24f1c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dd285d1-cf9d-5646-a446-ce41bd2e2458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532024} {:answer/id #uuid "c01060b2-f26a-567b-a1dd-ba792972b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532346} {:answer/id #uuid "ef41bf2a-c7d0-5536-922b-f7eb8c9ec0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{40\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532450} {:answer/id #uuid "f173cc96-34cf-5cea-9d45-09f0f19aa1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532455} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f80523-10c6-5bea-b36a-01b7f0de1f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532024} :answer-field/id #uuid "d460285e-96c9-5656-88a1-24adf10022f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c339d0f2-af34-5938-bbf8-d47914ddf165" :question/math-academy-id "q-138967" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-1,\\,y=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=1$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-t)={t}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({t}^{2}-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression ${t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot {t}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using direct integration we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{4}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-2)-(\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-1)] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{22}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{26}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{52\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a37727ba-408c-54ca-b44b-c58bf3c5ee21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397b7274-6f31-5ec6-8f5f-76084e482538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532049} {:answer/id #uuid "463add96-423b-5d1f-a988-936fb0b73d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2t(4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532075} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc93547-60e1-5394-aaaf-3f2640834b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\sqrt{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532179} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa4f95f-378d-5338-8b6f-85c27742f8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532249} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9b8633-af16-5b49-b623-797c047243a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532249} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ee6b17-46b2-5260-a155-51e284324ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d6c3d2a-2d0d-538a-8df1-4fa8a05510f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-138965" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t+1,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+1)\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d839e236-0c85-509d-9ef3-41872a77d0ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532747}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531930}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554680} :activity/id #uuid "b63b672a-110b-5025-89a1-a1f474d71b68" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00514e84-3144-5df4-82c2-33eb39ce62b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553865} {:answer/id #uuid "8db89be6-e547-5fa5-9888-b55fe82c1cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554170} {:answer/id #uuid "9273e530-04e8-5996-b68a-1f42eb0f608e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(2-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554192} {:answer/id #uuid "e88ae27f-5535-584b-9730-b1ae58287e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554372} {:answer/id #uuid "e892a90b-d1ba-521a-b1de-129de62c0978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554373} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e8aa5a-ac76-5fe7-8cbb-d1e2111d6174" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t/2},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}({4}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2e8bc30-41c9-56c8-a5b2-e047a033dedb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367465} {:answer/id #uuid "b854c123-c85d-5e04-a948-bd8a101d0416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{-t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367466} {:answer/id #uuid "14edfcb2-c8de-5378-b4d9-078fcd77b9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367467} {:answer/id #uuid "8922920d-3e40-5b79-b054-c8ad34ddd743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367468} {:answer/id #uuid "f50415c4-c627-581d-942c-cb7597564764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367467} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c091cf5-1213-5f71-95d4-647a3b748c8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1cc47049-1d82-526c-84a3-b9c51fd53a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-139026" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t},\\,y=2{e}^{-t},\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=-2$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})=2{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{-t})=-2{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t})}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{-t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{4({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2},$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "67d2476e-8b28-5c63-84c6-efc8f7cef93c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34815984-fefd-5798-bdd6-d8ac9e63495b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266603} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3baba4-8572-53a6-81aa-d3e89b039d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266984} {:answer/id #uuid "6f44b9c5-062e-5836-8fa5-9b241e49df01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267122} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5f9d3b-c7f5-5304-ad78-7cc3982df96e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267539} {:answer/id #uuid "b26a0858-4124-5ef1-b554-c41d2275431e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266603} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab978d17-dd39-5ce8-a339-813c29c490be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3645ded7-1477-514e-b282-7668816fc19c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139056" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3},\\,y=2t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "83dd259c-644a-5187-abca-3fbeaba6c996" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3256b67-7e99-5826-a95a-b55a197e4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184} :answer-field/id #uuid "12fa28de-d131-50b4-b0e1-7dd696b0e54f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f72f786-eb31-5b03-9051-ae70858e9f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-352035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt[4]{6}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "86a8d041-feed-5f21-a7aa-0238cf6d1240" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15a0040-721e-5583-80c4-6b4b6d161036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi(2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b596d95-5abf-5a25-8619-bd46ab687c7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "95891d65-20e2-5732-9bd4-e52bbf49b5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352088" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9479a2f7-46a4-5db5-a1e5-a589077eded2"}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553855}]}
